#
# name: long-form closure assignment
# `$name = closure(...) end`
#====================================================================


# design decisions for Section 11 (functions, closures, methods):
#
#   Two orthogonal axes to preserve in CaspJ:
#
#   1. Which SYNTACTIC FORM the developer used (setvar-verb):
#        setvar   — long form:  `$foo = function(...) ... end`
#        def      — sugar form: `function &foo(...) ... end`
#      Both do the same thing (create a callable, bind it to the variable);
#      CaspJ preserves which form was written.
#
#   2. Which KIND of callable was created (value-atom key):
#        {function: {params, body}}  — plain function (no scope capture)
#        {closure:  {params, body}}  — closure (captures the enclosing scope)
#        {method:   {params, body}}  — method (only ever appears via the def
#                                       verb; there is no long-form
#                                       `$foo = method(...)`)
#
#   Combined shape:  ["scope", <verb>, <name>, {<kind>: {params, body}}]
#
#   Params in V1: a list where each entry is either a plain string (bare
#   param name, no metadata) or an object `{name: "x", meta: [[k, v], ...]}`
#   (param with rich metadata). The `$` sigil is a use-site concern and
#   isn't stored on the param entry.
#
#   Rich metadata syntax: `$name: {class: :string, default: 5, ...}` — colon
#   after the param name, then a hash literal. Values in the metadata hash
#   are parsed as value-atoms; the runtime interprets them at call time
#   (`default:` re-runs per call, per parameter-defaults rule; `class:` is
#   a constant type constraint). `meta` is a list of `[key, value_atom]`
#   pairs — same shape convention as call-site `kw` and heredoc paren-block
#   kwargs.
#
#   Splat params: `*$args` (positional collector) and `**$opts` (kwargs
#   collector) at the param declaration site. Shape mirrors the rich-
#   metadata form with a `splat: true` or `kwsplat: true` flag:
#     *$args         -> {name: "args", splat: true}
#     **$opts        -> {name: "opts", kwsplat: true}
#     *$args: {...}  -> {name: "args", splat: true, meta: [...]}
#   Semantically the DECLARATION-side inverse of the call-site splat atoms
#   from Section 12 — declaration collects, call spreads.
#
# The composite `$rich_params = function(...) end` example that exercises every
# rich-param feature (metadata + splat + kwsplat + long-form assignment) is
# now a fixture above; the survey block previously here has been promoted.
